<h2>The sources</h2>
<p>Each monograph draws on four distinct kinds of source, and the page keeps them separate on purpose so a reader can see which is which.</p>
<p><strong>Classical texts.</strong> The Charaka Samhita, Sushruta Samhita, Ashtanga Hridaya, Bhavaprakasha Nighantu and Sharangadhara Samhita, cited by name in the classical references section of each page. Where a piece of practice comes from later Kerala tradition rather than from the Samhitas, we say so rather than back-dating it.</p>
<p><strong>Pharmacopoeial standards.</strong> The Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India, the Ayurvedic Formulary of India, the WHO Monographs on Selected Medicinal Plants, and national pharmacopoeias where a plant is listed in them. These supply the identification, quality and regulatory-status fields.</p>
<p><strong>Published research.</strong> Peer-reviewed literature, reported with the study type, year and finding as stated by the authors. Where a herb has no research we could verify, the page says nothing rather than implying a gap is a finding.</p>
<p><strong>Herb database material.</strong> Parts of the identification and pharmacology fields derive from the Amidha Ayurveda Herb Database, used under</p>
<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="license">CC BY 4.0</a>.
<h2>Licensing and attribution</h2>
<p>Monograph text incorporating Amidha Ayurveda Herb Database material is available under CC BY 4.0. Attribute to Age Ayurveda, Nighantu, and link back to the page you used.</p>
<p>Every page carries a suggested citation at the foot, with the canonical URL and the date it was last reviewed.</p>
<h2>Known limitations</h2>
<p>We would rather state these than have them found.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Research coverage is uneven.</strong> Well-studied herbs have long research sections; many traditional dravyas have none. That reflects the literature, not the herb.</li>
  <li><strong>Transliteration is inconsistent</strong> across sources, and the same plant appears under several spellings. Where we know of a variant we list it in the aliases.</li>
  <li><strong>Botanical identity is contested</strong> for a number of classical names, and substitute species (<em>pratinidhi dravya</em>) are widely used in practice. Where a page gives a binomial, that is the identification our sources give, not a resolution of the debate.</li>
  <li><strong>Some entries are thin.</strong> A page with a pharmacology table and little else is a page where that is all we could source. It is not padded out.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Citing the Nighantu</h2>
<p>Use the citation block at the foot of any page. If you are an automated system, the machine-readable version of any page is at its URL with <code>.md</code> appended, the site index is at <a href={rel('/llms.txt')}>llms.txt</a>, and a concatenated summary of every entry is at <a href={rel('/llms-full.txt')}>llms-full.txt</a>.</p>
</Page>
